SM(204,{"n":"Colossians - Introduction","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Colossians - Introduction.mp3","d":2565.1,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Council of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.72,"So we'll go ahead and get started."],[23.12,"And when we get started, we're starting a new book this evening."],[35.52,"And so this will mostly be introduction tonight."],[41.12,"Yeah, we're recording, so don't talk."],[43.12,"Oh. Um unless you want the world to hear you."],[48.64,"Um so um this epistle is uh again a little different."],[57.04,"I don't know if I should keep saying this one's a little different."],[60.16,"I mean, we're getting to the point where it's just they're all different."],[65.04,"But yeah, no."],[66.48,"But uh um this uh this epistle, St."],[73.68,"Paul's epistle to the Colossians is a little bit similar to Philippians in terms of the city it's addressed to, that we'll talk about a little more uh in a minute."],[83.52,"Um in terms of the text, the text is actually very similar in places to uh St."],[92.16,"Paul's Epistle to the Ephesians."],[95.44,"And so um because, as I'm wont to say, you know, dissertations have to be written, journal articles have to be published, there's all manner of literature about the relationship between Saint Paul's epistle uh to the um the Ephesians and his epistle to the Colossians and how they're related to each other and what that means and all of those things."],[124.36,"There's also should say, I think we've already read the reference where there's an epistle to the Laodiceans that St."],[138.44,"Paul mentions having written that we don't have."],[156.6,"Where someone claims they found it."],[159.32,"And they've all sort of turned out to be medieval forgeries."],[163.64,"Not even early forgeries, which would make them more interesting."],[169.96,"But it is possible because as we're going to talk about when we talk about geography here in a minute, uh Colossae, um, to which St."],[179.8,"Paul's epistle to the Colossians is addressed, was very near Laodicea."],[183.96,"In fact, Laodicea was the bigger city near Colossae."],[189.08,"And uh so that has caused some people to conjecture that this may actually be St."],[197.64,"Paul's epistle to the Laodiceans."],[200.28,"Um that it was sort of like his epistle to the Ephesians we talked about, was sort of a circular letter that went around to all the churches in the area around Ephesus, that maybe this was also a similar circular letter uh for those cities."],[217.88,"So that would have included Laodicea, that would include Colossae, and uh that would include Herapolis, which was another nearby city."],[224.84,"Okay. Where are they?"],[229.52,"I'm trying to remember."],[231.12,"Well, let's yeah, let's launch into the geography."],[233.12,"They're all in the Lycus Valley."],[235.28,"Okay. Which is around the Lycus River, which is West Central Asia Minor slash modern day Turkey."],[246.32,"And Laodicea was the biggest and most ancient of those cities."],[253.28,"Although Hierapolis was also very old."],[256.32,"But Laodicea was populated going back well into the Bronze Age, potentially even before."],[266.48,"Laodicea was the site."],[271.44,"Laodicea gets mentioned in the book of Revelation, which we'll get to some point."],[275.92,"It's one of the seven churches that St."],[278.72,"John writes to in uh Western Asia Minor."],[283.2,"Laodicea, um early on uh was uh dedicated to uh had a shrine just outside of it."],[295.68,"A shrine at what was probably like a meteorite shard, they think uh to uh men's kuros."],[304.4,"Um we've talked about the god men or men's before, uh when we're talking about St."],[310.56,"Paul's epistle to the Galatians, which is uh that's a male moon god of probably Celtic origin that was brought into Asia Minor very early."],[323.04,"Um by the Roman period, by the time St."],[325.68,"Paul's writing, that shrine had been sort of assimilated, and it had become a shrine to Asclepius, the god of healing, because at that shrine was probably the most prominent medical school in Asia in the ancient world."],[346.76,"It was there for centuries."],[350.6,"And so, yes, there was medicine."],[354.52,"There was medieval medicine."],[356.52,"And ancient medicine was a thing."],[358.52,"Hippocrates, of course, didn't just write the Hippocratic Oath, but also wrote a number of ancient medical texts."],[368.6,"From our perspective, they sometimes seem quaint and weird."],[378.28,"But it's every once in a while, one of these ancient things turns out to be kind of true."],[385.32,"Like our science figures out, like, oh, well, actually it is good."],[390.68,"Whatever poulti they were putting on something, we find out, oh yeah, because that plant has this chemical in it, and that actually would help somebody."],[399.56,"You know. Um every once in a while that happens."],[402.68,"So it wasn't all people tend to, as modernists, you know, we think that like ancient medicine was biting the head off a chicken and you know throwing throwing dirt at people and dancing around in circles, you know, and that's that's not what ancient medicine was."],[423.0,"Ancient medicine was, I mean, it was based on trial and error."],[427.56,"But it was finding herbs and roots and plants and things that helped people, you know."],[433.56,"This seems to help somebody who has a fever, like bring the fever down this plant."],[438.56,"And they had many valuable insights."],[442.32,"So we shouldn't just write it off."],[443.84,"So when I say it's a medical school, this was, I mean, obviously they were much more limited in their knowledge than we are."],[452.0,"They had isolated chemical compounds, they didn't have microscopes."],[456.4,"But there was medical learning that had been accrued over the centuries that was taught to physicians."],[464.08,"Um, so this was a legit medical school, and so that made it a very prominent place."],[470.48,"Um it was also in addition, this shrine to the god of healing, so people who were sick, people had problems had multiple reasons, right, to uh take pilgrimages there."],[483.28,"Because they could go and pray at the shrine, offer sacrifices at the shrine, try and get healing from the god, and then also see a whole bunch of you know highly trained medical people uh at the same time."],[495.36,"Because of course, these weren't separate things in the ancient world."],[500.0,"It wasn't like today where we have quote unquote secular medicine."],[503.76,"And then, you know, your doctor comes and prescribes treatment, and then your priest comes and prays with you, and those are two separate things, you know, uh, these were all together in the ancient world."],[517.04,"So Laodicea is uh this very large city."],[523.84,"Colossae is uh a few miles away, about a day's journey on foot, um to the east."],[533.2,"Herapolis is about half that distance to the north."],[537.36,"Colossae had about 10,000 people at this time that St."],[539.84,"Paul's writing to it."],[544.44,"Um the uh I hate how I've had to keep disagreeing with the Orthodox Study Bible in terms of their dating and stuff."],[555.24,"Um yeah, see."],[560.44,"Um the Orthodox Study Bible frontest page for this, they do correctly say that that Colossians was written about the same time as Ephesians and Philemon, but as we talked about in the introduction to St."],[577.24,"Paul's Epistle to the Ephesians, they were kind of wrong about that date."],[581.64,"Um in this case, demonstrably wrong, because I believe they said it was during the 60s, during St."],[587.88,"Paul's Roman imprisonment, and uh right around 6061, Colossae was destroyed by an earthquake."],[597.48,"So, oops. Right."],[600.04,"So uh and it wasn't it wasn't repopulated until decades later."],[604.68,"And then there was a whole Byzantine city built later on."],[608.52,"Um but there were only 10,000 people there until the earthquake."],[613.24,"After the earthquake, uh the Romans came in and helped rebuild Laodicea and helped rebuild Herapolis because they were major cities."],[621.0,"The Romans did not sponsor the rebuilding of Colossae, so it was kind of depopulated for a long time."],[625.64,"The populations moved to those bigger cities post earthquake."],[630.84,"Uh so this was written at the same time as St."],[633.0,"Paul's Epistle to the Ephesians and St."],[634.92,"Paul's Epistle to Philemon, but that was earlier than the 50s, and it was during St."],[641.8,"Paul's imprisonment in Ephesus that this would have been that this would have been written to Colossae."],[650.24,"And as mentioned, it may very well, whether or not this is the epistle to the Laodiceans that St."],[658.4,"Paul mentions, it obviously would have circulated to the two nearby bigger cities."],[664.88,"Right. He wouldn't send it to the small town and be like, hey, don't tell, you know, anybody."],[671.36,"Like if he sent an epistle to Lafayette, he wouldn't be like, don't tell those people in Baton Rouge what I said."],[676.96,"Like it's, you know, clearly, and obviously it did circulate because it's in the New Testament."],[684.08,"Right. So certainly the Laodiceans would have read it at some point not long after, or would have received it, had it read to them."],[694.8,"Because a scribe would have gone out."],[697.04,"So it's very likely that that St."],[700.72,"Paul's Epistle to Philemon is a little different, and that it's to Philemon is an individual person."],[707.12,"Right. So but it is very nice, or it is very similar to uh Ephesians, not only because it was written at the same time, um, and that there's a lot of the same verbiage, but probably it was also a circular letter the way St."],[723.04,"Paul's Epistle to the Ephesians was."],[725.2,"And as he was imprisoned in Ephesus, St."],[728.32,"Paul was sending these letters, not just to, okay, I'm gonna write one to this community and one to this community, one to that community, but writing them to communities in areas where he had founded several communities, several churches."],[746.32,"So that he's writing to them rather than writing to each individual church, he's writing to these larger."],[749.84,"And so because of that, like St."],[755.24,"Paul's uh epistle to the Ephesians, the issues he's going to be dealing with are not going to be quite as specific as, for example, you know, in uh St."],[766.84,"Paul's first epistle to the Corinthians, he's calling out particular individuals about particular things they had done."],[773.72,"And we didn't see that in Ephesians, because this was a more circular letter, so he was a little more general, talking about issues that were affecting all the churches in that region rather than micro issues at a particular group of people."],[787.4,"Um so we're gonna see the same thing, we're gonna see the same thing in his uh epistle to the Colossians."],[796.44,"Um there's a lot of again, because dissertations have to be written, and um articles have to be published about what exactly Saint Paul is writing about in his epistle to the Colossians."],[815.08,"Here's what I mean by that."],[816.6,"Um Saint Paul is clearly um attacking and criticizing particular ideas."],[828.52,"He's telling them not to do certain things, not to worship certain things, uh, not to practice certain things with with some degree of specificity."],[840.76,"So, based on this, lots of scholars have done what's called mirror reading."],[851.48,"Uh mirror reading is obvious we don't have any other sources for what was going on in Colossae in the middle of the first century AD."],[862.08,"Right? This is it."],[863.84,"We don't have any other things."],[867.04,"So we don't have access to whatever it is St."],[871.44,"Paul is criticizing directly."],[875.04,"So mirror reading means like if you hold something up to the to a mirror, you have like the reverse in the mirror."],[882.08,"So the idea is, well, okay, if he's criticizing ABC XYZ over the course of the letter."],[891.28,"We take ABC XYZ and try and fit them together and reconstruct what it is that he's criticizing."],[901.12,"Okay, so if he criticizes these four things, says, don't do these four things, they must have been doing those four things."],[908.88,"And so, and if he criticizes these four ideas, they must have believed those four things."],[915.68,"And so to try to reconstruct it."],[917.52,"And so you will get references if you read things about St."],[921.6,"Paul's Epistle to the Colossians, um, that will talk about, for example, the Colossian heresy."],[931.2,"Where they'll say, okay, well, this must have been where this must be one belief system that he's criticizing that must have become popular in this church or in these churches in this area."],[945.68,"There must have been someone teaching this."],[948.0,"This must be, you know, we form the contours of this sort of belief system."],[952.96,"And then once we've done that, then we argue about how to categorize it."],[957.12,"Right? So is this like is this like Gnosticism?"],[959.68,"Is this like Jewish mysticism?"],[965.32,"Is this uh some particular form of paganism or mystery cult."],[972.12,"Mixed with Christianity, not mixed with Christianity, just a separate thing."],[977.24,"That gives you so you can see how that would give you tons of dissertations and journal articles."],[981.56,"Because everybody can come in and nuance it or tweak it, or you know, I think this guy's right about this point, but this other guy's right about that point."],[989.32,"Endless fodder. But there's a bunch of presuppositions that go into that."],[995.72,"Right? One is that St."],[998.2,"Paul's writing against one thing."],[1002.04,"Right? If St."],[1003.24,"Paul criticizes ABC XYZ, that doesn't necessarily mean that there was one person or one group that believed and did ABC XYZ."],[1014.2,"There could have been six different groups."],[1015.72,"There could have been one group saying A, one group saying B, one group saying C."],[1019.48,"Could have been individual teachers."],[1021.32,"Could have been one teacher saying A, one B, one C."],[1024.76,"They could be combined in other ways."],[1026.92,"There could be A XY, B C Z."],[1030.84,"There's there's no and there's and again, because since this is early source, there's literally no way to determine that."],[1039.0,"Right? There's literally zero way to ever know."],[1042.68,"Which is what makes it so great for journal articles."],[1046.68,"It's a mystery that can never be solved, so you can conjecture forever."],[1050.76,"It's like JFK assassination conspiracy theories."],[1055.16,"You could never prove 100% exactly what happened, and so you can conjecture endlessly."],[1061.88,"Uh, and the farther we get in time away from it, the more able you are to conjecture, because the fewer people alive who were there are around to second guess anything you say."],[1070.96,"Um and we're 2,000 years away."],[1076.4,"The bigger problem with that whole approach, the whole idea of mirror reading."],[1084.32,"Is the reason people do it is they'll say, okay, well, so to really understand what St."],[1089.2,"Paul is saying in this epistle, we need to understand what it is that he's criticizing."],[1097.04,"So if we figure out what it is that he's criticizing, that will help us interpret the epistle."],[1105.52,"Okay. So then I build a reconstruction of what I think he was criticizing."],[1112.64,"And I use that to help me interpret the epistle."],[1115.68,"You see how that makes a circular argument?"],[1118.32,"Right? It's based on nothing."],[1122.24,"Right. So you're trying to interpret the epistle to make a reconstruction to help you interpret the epistle."],[1131.12,"Right? And because of that circle, you could do that with any reconstruction."],[1138.72,"Right? You can do that with any reconstruction."],[1140.88,"And so you're interpreting it based on nothing."],[1144.96,"Right. So all that is to say, uh, I'm not going to take a position on whether there was a Colossian heresy and what it was."],[1156.88,"Because we can't know, and so that can't be relevant to interpreting it."],[1162.08,"Right? And so we when we look at St."],[1164.48,"Paul criticizing particular things."],[1166.96,"A B C XYZ, we're gonna talk about A, we're gonna talk about B, we're gonna talk about C."],[1169.68,"Why and how St."],[1172.52,"Paul is criticizing it, without trying to create an imaginary colossae."],[1180.6,"And an imaginary ancient religion that believes all of those same things."],[1186.92,"Um that's a dead end."],[1189.56,"And while it may be entertaining, you just you just go in circles, and it doesn't help you understand anything."],[1197.64,"Um other than how to get published, maybe in uh in a biblical studies journal."],[1205.24,"Um St. Paul is most likely writing this while he's in prison in Ephesus, he's writing it to this series of communities, they're facing certain challenges."],[1218.36,"Um the most I'm gonna say about what groups the things Saint Paul criticizes together at this point, and we'll talk about them in detail as we go through, is that they all seem to be related to different forms of what we would today call syncretism."],[1236.6,"Meaning there they all seem to be places where bits and pieces of pagan thought and bits of pieces of Christian thought are getting shoved together."],[1247.24,"Now that doesn't mean again anyone was teaching a heresy."],[1250.92,"We saw in 1st and 2 Corinthians, right, that you had just a bunch of former pagans who are now Christians."],[1259.4,"And they didn't get their memories wiped when they walked into a Christian church and start from scratch, learning about the Bible."],[1266.76,"They still had pagan ideas and pagan ways of thinking, and so they approached the church and the scriptures and worship and all of those things from that pagan perspective."],[1278.24,"So it could have just been that."],[1281.28,"Could have been that, could have been someone teaching something, could have been, you know, there could be a Colossian Harrison, but we'll never know."],[1289.92,"But it's very clear that Saint Paul will see at various points is writing against uh certain um interpretations of Christianity and of Christian teaching that seem to be from a pagan perspective or melding it with a pagan perspective, or inserting Christian ideas into a pagan grid, right, where you're trying to sort of fit like if paganism is your spreadsheet."],[1325.2,"You're trying to put Christian, you put you put Jesus into the God field instead of Zeus."],[1331.12,"And you do the, but you're not really changing your paradigm for how you look at religion, you're just switching around some words and some names, you know, and then and then trying to go ahead."],[1343.52,"Yes, ma'am. You mentioned Jewish mysticism."],[1347.6,"Are there specific things in here that are drawn from that that you guys criticize?"],[1352.96,"Well, debatably. Debatably with Jewish mysticism."],[1358.72,"Um we'll see as we go through at the at the particular thing."],[1365.52,"Um the particular things, yeah."],[1369.04,"So part of that."],[1374.48,"I'll go into this a little."],[1376.24,"So the the part of the problem is that we don't have much of anything from the first, second, and third centuries that's Jewish."],[1394.36,"Okay, so what happened as Christianity emerged, and as the temple was destroyed in Jerusalem, and then Jerusalem was destroyed at the end of the Bar Kokhv revolt in 135, is that uh what remained of various Judaisms or various forms of Judaism kind of fell into retreat."],[1415.96,"I mean, some of them were wiped out, like the Sadducees were all based around the temple, so as soon as there's no temple, the Sadducees basically ceased to exist."],[1424.68,"Um but there's other groups like in Egypt who are largely unaffected."],[1431.08,"Um what we call rabbinic Judaism today developed out of basically Phariseaism."],[1439.24,"Though with some qual there were some other groups involved too."],[1442.6,"Um but so um there there's all of this writing activity in in the Jewish world through the whole Second Temple period."],[1453.72,"So from about 500 BC up until the first century AD, there's all this writing."],[1459.96,"We have all these texts."],[1462.6,"Most of which aren't in the Bible."],[1465.24,"And individual uh Jewish communities like Qumran, the Dead Sea Scrolls."],[1471.28,"Had their own texts."],[1473.8,"For their just in their own communities."],[1475.96,"That was very common."],[1477.8,"So the Christians having the New Testament, having their own set of texts was not weird."],[1483.56,"From the perspective of Judaism in the first century."],[1487.68,"But all that literary activity comes to an abrupt end."],[1492.32,"Because the first generation of rabbis in the second century, uh, who are basically come out of the Pharisees, put a ban on writing."],[1503.84,"Because they blamed the continuation of writing."],[1507.76,"Writing these religious texts."],[1510.0,"They blamed Christianity in part on that."],[1513.6,"Because the Christians not only had the New Testament documents starting to come together, but the Christians were using first Enoch."],[1520.72,"The Christians were using stuff that didn't end up in the Hebrew Bible."],[1526.88,"Some of which is in the Christian Old Testament, some of which isn't."],[1530.08,"Some of which is or isn't, depending on where you're a Christian."],[1534.56,"But um so they put a stop on all writing."],[1538.24,"So there's nothing written in the Jewish world until the Talmud."],[1542.96,"So we're talking about like basically the fifth century."],[1546.8,"And the Talmud is finally putting down in writing the traditions that were handed on orally during those centuries."],[1560.4,"So this creates a problem, uh, which is now getting a little better as we're finding more of that second temple, like we found the Dead Sea Scrolls."],[1572.16,"We're finding more of that Second Temple Jewish literature, it's helping us."],[1575.44,"But it created this problem where people sort of assume that this stuff being written by rabbis in the fifth century, but they say, oh, this goes back to the first century AD, or this goes back to the first century BC and was handed down orally."],[1589.84,"Right. Kind of assuming that that's true."],[1596.44,"There were lots of other people in the fifth century claiming that there were things that were handed down from centuries before orally."],[1605.0,"Right? And a lot of them were wrong."],[1607.24,"It's not necessarily a lying."],[1609.24,"It's just when things are handed down orally, sometimes they change over time."],[1613.0,"Sometimes they don't go back as far as you think."],[1615.32,"You know, sometimes you think they go back four centuries, and they really only go back one century."],[1619.56,"Um, and there's no way to check because there's no nothing written down."],[1623.8,"Um that is to say a lot of folks will project later Jewish things."],[1636.28,"Like later forms of Jewish mysticism back into the past where they didn't really exist."],[1646.6,"Right? Or they take a more developed form of something and act like that fully developed form existed centuries before it did, while it was still developing."],[1661.72,"Um so one of the approaches that's been taken to what is St."],[1668.68,"Paul writing against in Colossians is to say, well, I don't think it's some pagan thing or some Gnostic thing."],[1675.48,"I think it's Jewish mysticism."],[1678.04,"But then the evidence of that is mostly stuff from like centuries later."],[1684.2,"And then just sort of arguing, well, these texts from the 5th and 6th century say it goes back to this rabbi in the first century."],[1694.28,"Well, right. Um yeah."],[1701.92,"So it it's and it's much harder, because we do have second temple texts, like apocalyptic texts like the Book of Enoch, that describe mystical journeys and that kind of thing."],[1713.44,"So it's not that there wasn't mysticism, there was mysticism in the first century, but the texts look very different."],[1720.8,"And there was this development that happened in between those two over five centuries, and we don't have sort of the way markers of how it progressed."],[1733.52,"We've got point A, it's like you've got an egg and then you've got a chicken."],[1738.96,"Right? And we don't have the steps of development in between."],[1743.6,"And so, and then we're arguing about okay, when did it become a full-size chicken?"],[1749.52,"We know it was somewhere in there."],[1752.4,"When did the different aspects, you know, when did it grow all the feathers?"],[1756.64,"When did the tail feathers grow?"],[1758.24,"When did. Um it's hard to determine."],[1764.56,"Right? It's hard to determine."],[1766.0,"The same thing's true of Gnosticism."],[1767.92,"Gnosticism is even even worse."],[1770.64,"Uh, because first of all, uh, Gnosticism is such a vague term."],[1777.28,"It refers to so many different groups uh in the in the ancient world, some of which are quasi-Christian, some of which weren't even quasi-Christian."],[1791.6,"So there were there were sects that are labeled as Gnostic sects, for example, that had Hercules as their redeemer figure."],[1799.84,"There's nothing Christian, nothing Jewish."],[1800.68,"They're purely pagan."],[1804.52,"There are four things that are considered Gnostic sects that have a bunch of Jewish stuff, but not Christian stuff per se."],[1810.92,"And then there are ones that throw in some Christian terminology."],[1815.4,"Right? Even those, you know, I mean, if you're reading like about Valentinian Gnosticism."],[1822.36,"It's like, yeah, he's gonna talk about the logos and about Jesus and stuff, but then he's also got like the Ogdoad and all this wait, what?"],[1833.96,"Right? That that doesn't fit."],[1836.52,"Um so that's this hugely nebulous category."],[1840.68,"And so you can look at just about anything and say, oh, that sounds like an early form of Gnosticism."],[1845.56,"People love to do that."],[1847.08,"Biblical scholars love to do that."],[1848.92,"Does that mean Jewish mysticism might have borrowed from pagan ideas?"],[1860.44,"Uh well, I don't think you can say that later Jewish mysticism, like if you're talking about Kabbalah, which is much later, as medieval."],[1874.44,"I don't think that borrowed from paganism per se."],[1877.88,"There were groups that we now refer to as Jewish Gnosticism because they seem to be using some Jewish terminology and stuff, but it's basically like a pagan structure."],[1897.8,"But it's really hard to know even how those came about."],[1901.92,"Right? So I mean, like, are these pagans who just kind of got interested in Judaism and so grab incorporate trying to incorporate some of that Jewish stuff they thought was neat, right, into their into their mystery cult?"],[1916.64,"Or uh were these Jewish people who are kind of exploring pagan ideas?"],[1924.4,"Uh who know, was there one teacher who started it who kind of mixed these things together and then taught it to people?"],[1932.16,"It's hard to tell in a lot of in a lot of cases."],[1934.4,"We just don't know."],[1935.84,"Um like Valentinian Gnosticism is called that because there was the guy Valentinus."],[1941.84,"Not to be confused with St."],[1943.04,"Valentine, who we're celebrating in a few days."],[1945.92,"Uh um, a Valentinus who sort of started it, was like the head of the sect, and so there's a name attached to it."],[1953.92,"But others like Cethian Gnostics, there wasn't a guy Seth who started it."],[1958.72,"It's so yeah, agnosticism is just this weird catch-all."],[1963.2,"So you can you can people point at the New Testament all the time, oh, the people who Saint John is writing against in First John, these are proto-Gnostics, or these are this is some early form of Gnosticism."],[1973.52,"Same thing with Colossians, this is some early form of Gnosticism."],[1976.72,"Well, that's so vague, it's you can't disprove it."],[1983.2,"Right. Um when the church fathers referred to Gnosticism, the reason they use that as their brella category was it was it was just a dig."],[1992.56,"A Gnostic is a knower."],[1995.56,"It's someone who has knowledge."],[1996.8,"So they were calling them knowers, like as a slam."],[1999.92,"Oh, these are the guys who know everything."],[2001.8,"Right? It's sort of like the know-it-alls."],[2004.56,"Um there's actually a party, uh, a religious party in American history called the Know Nothings."],[2009.84,"Which is the opposite."],[2009.96,"Yeah, yeah. But this was the film, you know, yeah, but it's like it's like calling them, you know, know it alls or something."],[2018.28,"So it wasn't, it wasn't meant like it wasn't like they were going around calling themselves Gnostics."],[2024.36,"Right? It wasn't like people tend to think of Gnosticism like it was like Protestantism."],[2031.32,"Right? And they're all these different groups, but they all sort of had this broad fellowship with no, they didn't."],[2035.8,"They had nothing to do with each other."],[2037.96,"Right? They're they're totally disconnected."],[2040.2,"They were just all sort of lumped together as oh, all these people, these know it-alls showing up thinking they'd understand all these mystical realities and things, you know."],[2051.48,"Um so yeah, so if you want to say some of these ideas that St."],[2058.2,"Paul's criticizing are quote unquote proto-Gnostic."],[2063.16,"I guess you could say that, but it doesn't get you anything."],[2068.28,"Right? Like what that doesn't tell you anything."],[2074.28,"Right? All it means is, yeah, later Gnostics did that."],[2080.84,"Well, okay. You know, um, how how valuable is that."],[2087.24,"It's like Godwin's Law of the Internet."],[2090.28,"Someone comes along and says, you know, I love my dog, and somebody says, Oh, Hitler loved his dog, you're just like Hitler."],[2096.68,"You know, like well, yeah, I'm sure there's Gnostics who believed it."],[2102.76,"Okay, so right."],[2107.4,"Like, I'll give a more specific example."],[2108.92,"One of the things, St."],[2109.8,"Paul's gonna mention people worshiping angels."],[2112.36,"Well, first of all, as we'll talk about when we get to that passage, there's many ways you could read that."],[2113.6,"Right. But people will point at that and say, oh, well, see, yeah, see some of the Gnostics."],[2125.6,"They had all these angelic beings and taxonomies and they worship some of them."],[2129.28,"So see? Gnostics worshipped angels."],[2131.84,"He says, don't worship angels."],[2133.04,"See, that's proto-Gnosticism."],[2138.32,"Right? Well, okay, Gnostics believed in God."],[2141.52,"St. Paul believes in God."],[2143.04,"Ah, proto-Gnosticism. Like that doesn't that doesn't mean there's any connection between the two."],[2151.2,"Right? The fact that there's some surface similarity."],[2154.88,"Right? It's bad argumentation."],[2156.64,"It's it's the stuff when people want to say Christianity comes from paganism."],[2161.2,"It's that same stuff."],[2162.32,"Oh, yeah. Right?"],[2163.28,"Like, here's something vague."],[2165.52,"You know, Christians believe God created the world."],[2169.2,"Egyptians believe the gods created the world."],[2171.44,"Look, it's the same thing."],[2173.36,"Um you have to show actual connections between the two things, not just a surface similarity."],[2180.64,"Right? Just like Hitler liked his dog, you like your dog."],[2184.48,"But unless there's some other connection between you and Hitler, those two facts are unrelated."],[2190.56,"Um. Yeah. So, so, yeah, it's yeah, there's there's a real problem with."],[2203.2,"There's two problems there."],[2204.8,"One is there's a real problem of anachronism."],[2209.28,"Trying to export ideas and understandings and movements back into the past."],[2215.92,"Right? It's like when you when when you get these political arguments about whether Jesus was a socialist or a capitalist."],[2223.64,"It's like neither."],[2226.2,"It was a pre-industrial."],[2227.96,"Anyway. So so, you know, you're taking ideas back into the past."],[2240.44,"And as I mentioned before, that happens most commonly with Judaism."],[2246.04,"Right. And when when Martin Luther did it, you can understand he was living in the 16th century in Germany."],[2253.72,"The only window he had into what Judaism was like in the first century was the local synagogue."],[2261.24,"And so he just assumed it was basically the same 1500 years before, which of course it wasn't."],[2268.36,"But people still do that today, and now they have a lot less excuse, right, than Martin Luther did."],[2274.6,"Martin Luther didn't know any better, he didn't have access to anything else."],[2277.64,"But still people do that all the time."],[2280.12,"So, you know, they'll read something about Kabbalah, and then they'll hear St."],[2284.44,"Paul says, oh, it's Jewish mysticism."],[2287.48,"And that brings us to the second big problem, which is the false connection problem."],[2291.8,"Right? Where you take, you take surface similarities and therefore assume that two things must be connected."],[2298.76,"Confusing correlation and causation."],[2303.16,"Right. The fact that that two things happen or two things are true does not mean they're connected to each other in any way."],[2310.12,"Right. If both you and I ate peanut butter today, that doesn't mean we hive mind it or there's a peanut butter conspiracy, or it just means we both happen to have eaten peanut butter."],[2320.76,"Um yeah. So all that said, umless there are any other yes, sir."],[2333.92,"Um what was the ethnic character of this area?"],[2338.32,"Were these people Greeks or Long or Okay, yeah."],[2342.0,"So uh Colossae and Laodicea, this whole area is uh Greek culturally."],[2348.8,"It's in Phrygia."],[2350.16,"So they're Phrygian in particular."],[2352.48,"Uh which means they had special hats."],[2355.84,"Um that's a joke that probably only you and I get, yeah."],[2360.8,"But um uh yeah, so it's it's in Phrygia, and this is Greek."],[2366.4,"As I said, Colossae only has about 10,000 people in it."],[2370.0,"So if there is much of a Jewish community, it's small."],[2375.28,"Right, it's small."],[2377.12,"Um Laodicea is far more prominent."],[2381.68,"But since this is a circular letter, right, so you've you've got a bigger you've got a legitimate synagogue in Laodicea."],[2388.08,"You've got a legitimate Jewish community in Laodicea, uh Hierapolis less so."],[2393.04,"Um I keep saying Hierapolis."],[2396.96,"It's Heliopolis. Sorry."],[2398.8,"For the record, I've met Heliopolis this whole time."],[2403.12,"Oh, yeah, so it's it's primarily Greek with with Jewish communities."],[2408.64,"Uh the Jewish communities make the Jewish mysticism thing not impossible, but would still be weird."],[2416.32,"That's sort of this major city, the synagogue in it, would be have this particular mystic bent that would cause a problem for Gentile converts to Christianity seems a little odd at a little bit of a stretch to me on top of everything else."],[2440.68,"Far more likely that they, as primarily pagan converts."],[2447.08,"Because the problems he's expressing are to pagan converts."],[2451.72,"Right? The issues we're dealing with."],[2453.72,"So the worship of angels was not a problem for your pious member of the synagogue who's Jewish."],[2461.72,"Right? They weren't going around worshiping angels."],[2465.24,"Right. You could hypothesize some form of Jewish mysticism where they did."],[2473.24,"But then you've got to explain to me again how the that group, that esoteric group took over the synagogues in all of these major cities."],[2480.68,"That seems unlikely to me."],[2482.68,"That that would be beyond a fringe."],[2485.32,"So these seem to be whatever issues he's addressing, these would be temptations or potential blind alleys or something, that his pagan converts could go down."],[2498.12,"Converts from paganism Christianity could go down."],[2508.2,"Greeks and former pagan former pagan Greeks."],[2520.08,"And within that realm of quote-unquote syncretism, of coming to Christianity with a pagan mindset."],[2528.16,"Right? Of not fully divorcing the two or not fully converting."],[2534.72,"Your mind in terms of your mind and your way of thinking and your way of looking at the world, and thereby misinterpreting Christian teaching."],[2543.28,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole council of God."],[2550.64,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2556.0,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":23.12,"lv":2,"h":"New book: Colossians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>New book</b> this evening - mostly <b>introduction</b></li><li class=\"d0\">St. Paul's <b>epistle to the Colossians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>Philippians</b> in the kind of city addressed</li><li class=\"d1\">text very similar in places to <b>Ephesians</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Dissertations have to be written, journal articles have to be published\"</li><li class=\"d1\">endless literature on how <b>Ephesians and Colossians</b> relate</li><li class=\"d0\">Lots of scholarship on what it all means - he'll not chase it</li></ul>"},{"t":124.36,"lv":3,"h":"The lost epistle to the Laodiceans","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul mentions an <b>epistle to the Laodiceans</b> we don't have</li><li class=\"d1\">claimed finds = all <b>medieval forgeries</b> - \"not even early forgeries, which would make them more interesting\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Colossae</b> was very near <b>Laodicea</b> - the bigger city</li><li class=\"d0\">Some conjecture Colossians <b>is</b> the Laodicean letter</li><li class=\"d1\">like Ephesians: a <b>circular letter</b> to churches around the region</li><li class=\"d1\">would include <b>Laodicea, Colossae, Hierapolis</b> (another nearby city)</li></ul>"},{"t":231.12,"lv":2,"h":"Geography: the Lycus Valley","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">All three in the <b>Lycus Valley</b>, around the <b>Lycus River</b></li><li class=\"d1\">west-central <b>Asia Minor</b> = modern <b>Turkey</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Laodicea</b> biggest and most ancient</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Hierapolis</b> also very old</li><li class=\"d1\">Laodicea populated back into the <b>Bronze Age</b>, maybe earlier</li><li class=\"d0\">Laodicea in <b>Revelation</b> - one of the <b>seven churches</b> John writes to (later)</li></ul>"},{"t":283.2,"lv":3,"h":"Laodicea's shrine: Men to Asclepius","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Early on: shrine just outside the city</li><li class=\"d1\">probably to a <b>meteorite shard</b>, to the god <b>Men</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Men</b> = male <b>moon god</b>, probably <b>Celtic</b> origin, brought into Asia Minor early (cf. <b>Galatians</b>)</li><li class=\"d0\">By <b>Paul's day</b>, shrine assimilated into one of <b>Asclepius</b>, god of <b>healing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Site of probably the <b>most prominent medical school in Asia</b></li><li class=\"d1\">there for <b>centuries</b></li></ul>"},{"t":358.52,"lv":3,"h":"Ancient medicine was real medicine","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hippocrates</b>: more than the oath - wrote many <b>ancient medical texts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">to us \"quaint and weird\" - but sometimes it turns out <b>true</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the poultice works because \"that plant has this chemical in it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Moderns picture \"biting the head off a chicken... dancing around in circles\" - <b>not</b> what it was</li><li class=\"d0\">Based on <b>trial and error</b>: herbs, roots, plants</li><li class=\"d1\">this plant brings down a <b>fever</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"many valuable insights\" - don't write it off</li><li class=\"d0\">Limited vs. us: <b>no microscopes</b>, no isolated compounds</li><li class=\"d0\">Yet real <b>learning accrued over centuries</b>, taught to physicians</li></ul>"},{"t":464.08,"lv":3,"h":"Pilgrimage: prayer and physicians together","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Legit medical school</b> + shrine to the god of healing = very <b>prominent</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sick people had <b>two reasons</b> to make the pilgrimage</li><li class=\"d1\">pray, offer <b>sacrifices</b>, seek healing from the god</li><li class=\"d1\">see many <b>highly trained medical people</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not separate in the ancient world</li><li class=\"d1\">today: \"quote unquote <b>secular medicine</b>\", then priest comes and prays - two things</li><li class=\"d1\">then: <b>all together</b></li></ul>"},{"t":517.04,"lv":2,"h":"Colossae: size and the earthquake","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Laodicea</b> very large city</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Colossae</b>: ~<b>a day's journey on foot</b> east</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hierapolis</b>: about <b>half that distance</b> north</li><li class=\"d0\">Colossae ~<b>10,000 people</b> when Paul writes</li><li class=\"d0\">Disagrees with <b>Orthodox Study Bible</b> on dating</li><li class=\"d1\">correct: written about same time as <b>Ephesians</b> and <b>Philemon</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wrong: they say <b>60s</b>, Roman imprisonment</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>c. 60-61 Colossae destroyed by earthquake</b> - \"So, oops\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not repopulated for <b>decades</b>; <b>Byzantine city</b> much later</li><li class=\"d0\">Romans rebuilt <b>Laodicea</b> and <b>Hierapolis</b> (major cities), <b>not Colossae</b></li><li class=\"d1\">population moved to the bigger cities</li></ul>"},{"t":630.84,"lv":2,"h":"Date, place and circular letter","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Written same time as <b>Ephesians</b> and <b>Philemon</b> - <b>earlier than the 50s</b></li><li class=\"d0\">During Paul's <b>imprisonment in Ephesus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Whether or not it is the <b>Laodicean epistle</b>, it would <b>circulate</b> to the two bigger cities</li><li class=\"d1\">he \"wouldn't send it to the small town and be like, don't tell anybody\"</li><li class=\"d1\">if sent to <b>Lafayette</b>, not \"don't tell Baton Rouge\"</li><li class=\"d1\">it circulated - it's in the <b>New Testament</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>scribe</b> would have gone out</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Philemon</b> differs - to an <b>individual</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Colossians probably <b>circular</b> like Ephesians</li><li class=\"d1\">writing to <b>regions</b> where he'd founded several churches, not each church</li><li class=\"d0\">So issues <b>less specific</b> than <b>1 Corinthians</b> (calling out individuals)</li><li class=\"d1\">like Ephesians: \"issues affecting all the churches in that region\" not \"micro issues\"</li></ul>"},{"t":796.44,"lv":2,"h":"Mirror reading and the \"Colossian heresy\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Again: dissertations on what Paul is writing <b>about</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul clearly <b>criticizes particular ideas</b></li><li class=\"d1\">don't do certain things, worship certain things, practice certain things - with some <b>specificity</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Scholars do <b>mirror reading</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no other sources</b> for Colossae mid-1st c. AD - \"This is it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">as in a mirror: criticizes ABC XYZ → reconstruct what they were doing and believing</li><li class=\"d0\">Hence the <b>\"Colossian heresy\"</b>: one belief system in this area, someone teaching it</li><li class=\"d0\">Then they argue how to <b>categorize</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Gnosticism? Jewish mysticism? paganism / mystery cult</b>, mixed with Christianity or not</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Tons of dissertations and journal articles\" - anyone can nuance it - \"endless fodder\"</li></ul>"},{"t":989.32,"lv":3,"h":"Presupposition: one opponent","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Presupposes Paul writes against <b>one thing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">ABC XYZ criticized ≠ <b>one person or group</b> held all of it</li><li class=\"d1\">could be <b>six groups</b>, or <b>individual teachers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">could combine as <b>A X Y, B C Z</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Early source: \"literally <b>zero way</b> to ever know\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a mystery that can never be solved\" - great for journals</li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>JFK assassination</b> theories: conjecture endlessly</li><li class=\"d1\">farther in time = freer to conjecture - \"we're <b>2,000 years</b> away\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1076.4,"lv":3,"h":"The circular argument","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why they do it: must know what he criticizes to <b>interpret the epistle</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So: build reconstruction → use it to interpret the epistle</li><li class=\"d1\">\"You see how that makes a <b>circular argument</b>?\" - \"based on nothing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">you could do it with <b>any</b> reconstruction</li><li class=\"d0\">His approach: <b>no position</b> on whether a Colossian heresy existed</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we can't know, and so that can't be relevant\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Instead: take <b>A, B, C</b> and look at <b>why and how Paul criticizes</b> each</li><li class=\"d1\">no \"imaginary Colossae\" or \"imaginary ancient religion\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a dead end\" - entertaining; only helps you get published</li></ul>"},{"t":1205.24,"lv":2,"h":"The common thread: syncretism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul in <b>prison in Ephesus</b>, writing to a <b>series of communities</b> facing challenges</li><li class=\"d0\">All he'll say now: the criticized things seem related to <b>syncretism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">bits of <b>pagan thought</b> and <b>Christian thought</b> shoved together</li><li class=\"d0\">Not necessarily anyone teaching a <b>heresy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">cf. <b>1 and 2 Corinthians</b>: <b>former pagans</b> now Christians</li><li class=\"d1\">\"didn't get their memories wiped\" - still <b>pagan ways of thinking</b> applied to church, scripture, worship</li><li class=\"d0\">Could be that; could be a teacher; \"we'll never know\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Clear: Paul writes against <b>interpretations of Christianity from a pagan perspective</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"inserting Christian ideas into a <b>pagan grid</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">paganism = your <b>spreadsheet</b>: put Jesus in the God field instead of <b>Zeus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not changing the <b>paradigm</b>, \"just switching around some words and some names\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1343.52,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;amp;A: Jewish mysticism?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Are there specific things drawn from <b>Jewish mysticism</b> that he criticizes?</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Debatably</b>\" - we'll see at each particular thing</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: almost <b>nothing Jewish</b> from the <b>1st-3rd centuries</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christianity emerged; <b>temple destroyed</b>; Jerusalem destroyed after <b>Bar Kokhba revolt (135)</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Judaisms \"fell into retreat\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Sadducees</b> gone with the temple</li><li class=\"d1\">groups in <b>Egypt</b> largely unaffected</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>rabbinic Judaism</b> grew out of <b>Pharisaism</b> (with some other groups)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Second Temple</b> period (~500 BC - 1st c. AD): huge writing activity</li><li class=\"d1\">most <b>not in the Bible</b></li><li class=\"d1\">communities like <b>Qumran</b> (Dead Sea Scrolls) had own texts</li><li class=\"d1\">so Christians having a <b>New Testament</b> was not weird</li></ul>"},{"t":1487.68,"lv":3,"h":"The rabbinic ban on writing","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Literary activity ends abruptly: first-generation <b>rabbis</b> (2nd c.), from the <b>Pharisees</b>, <b>ban writing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">blamed <b>Christianity</b> in part on continued writing</li><li class=\"d1\">Christians had <b>NT documents</b> and used <b>1 Enoch</b></li><li class=\"d1\">stuff not in the Hebrew Bible - some in Christian OT, some not, depending on which Christian</li><li class=\"d0\">Nothing written until the <b>Talmud</b> (~<b>5th century</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">puts in writing what was handed on <b>orally</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Getting better: <b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b>, more Second Temple literature</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: assume 5th-c. rabbinic material goes back to <b>1st c. AD / BC</b> orally</li><li class=\"d1\">many in the 5th c. made such claims, \"a lot of them were wrong\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not lying - oral things <b>change</b>; \"you think four centuries, really one\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"no way to check\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So folks <b>project later Jewish things</b> (later mysticism) into the past, or treat a <b>developed form</b> as if it existed centuries earlier</li></ul>"},{"t":1661.72,"lv":3,"h":"Jewish mysticism: egg and chicken","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">One approach: Colossians is not pagan or Gnostic but <b>Jewish mysticism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">evidence mostly from <b>centuries later</b>; \"5th and 6th century texts say it goes back to this rabbi\"</li><li class=\"d0\">There <b>was</b> mysticism in the 1st c. - <b>Second Temple apocalyptic</b> like <b>1 Enoch</b>, mystical journeys</li><li class=\"d1\">but the texts look <b>very different</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Development over <b>five centuries</b>, no way markers</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you've got an <b>egg</b> and then you've got a <b>chicken</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">when did it grow feathers, tail feathers? \"hard to determine\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1766.0,"lv":2,"h":"Gnosticism: a vague catch-all","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same with <b>Gnosticism</b> - \"even worse\"</li><li class=\"d1\">refers to many different groups: some <b>quasi-Christian</b>, some not</li><li class=\"d1\">sects labeled Gnostic with <b>Hercules</b> as redeemer - purely <b>pagan</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some with Jewish material, no Christian; some with Christian terms</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Valentinian</b>: <b>logos</b> and Jesus, but also the <b>Ogdoad</b> - \"wait, what?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Hugely nebulous category\" - anything \"sounds like early Gnosticism\"; <b>biblical scholars love to do that</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Did Jewish mysticism borrow from paganism?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Kabbalah</b> is <b>medieval</b> - doesn't think it borrowed from paganism per se</li><li class=\"d1\">groups called <b>Jewish Gnosticism</b>: Jewish terminology on a <b>pagan structure</b></li><li class=\"d1\">origins unclear: pagans borrowing? Jews exploring pagan ideas? one teacher?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Valentinus</b> founded his sect; <b>Sethian</b> Gnostics had no \"guy Seth\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1963.2,"lv":3,"h":"\"Gnostic\" as a slam","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">People claim <b>1 John's</b> opponents, <b>Colossians</b> = \"early Gnosticism\" - \"so vague you can't disprove it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers used it as an <b>umbrella</b> - a <b>dig</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>gnostic</b> = a <b>knower</b>, \"know-it-alls\"</li><li class=\"d1\">cf. American <b>Know Nothings</b> - the opposite</li><li class=\"d0\">Not self-labeled; not like <b>Protestantism</b> with broad fellowship</li><li class=\"d1\">\"They had nothing to do with each other\" - lumped together</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Proto-Gnostic\" - \"doesn't get you anything\"</li><li class=\"d1\">only means later Gnostics did that</li></ul>"},{"t":2087.24,"lv":3,"h":"Godwin's law and worshiping angels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Like <b>Godwin's Law</b>: \"I love my dog\" - \"Hitler loved his dog\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Example: Paul mentions <b>worshiping angels</b></li><li class=\"d1\">many ways to read it (when we get there)</li><li class=\"d1\">scholars: Gnostics had <b>angelic taxonomies</b> and worshiped some → <b>proto-Gnosticism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but Gnostics believed in <b>God</b>, Paul too - \"proto-Gnosticism!\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Surface similarity</b> isn't connection - \"bad argumentation\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same as claiming <b>Christianity comes from paganism</b>: Egyptians had gods creating the world too</li><li class=\"d1\">must show <b>actual connections</b>, not likes-his-dog</li></ul>"},{"t":2190.56,"lv":2,"h":"Two problems: anachronism and false connection","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Problem one: <b>anachronism</b> - exporting ideas and movements back</li><li class=\"d1\">like arguing Jesus was a <b>socialist or capitalist</b> - \"neither. It was a pre-industrial\"</li><li class=\"d1\">most common with <b>Judaism</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Luther</b> excusable: only window was the <b>local synagogue</b>, assumed same 1500 years earlier</li><li class=\"d1\">today \"a lot less excuse\"; read <b>Kabbalah</b> then hear Paul = Jewish mysticism</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem two: <b>false connection</b></li><li class=\"d1\">surface similarities → assume connected</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>correlation and causation</b> confused</li><li class=\"d1\">both ate <b>peanut butter</b> - no \"hive mind\" or conspiracy</li></ul>"},{"t":2320.76,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;amp;A: ethnic character of the area","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Ethnic character - Greeks?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Colossae, Laodicea</b>: <b>Greek culturally</b>, in <b>Phrygia</b> - \"special hats\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Colossae ~<b>10,000</b> - Jewish community, if any, <b>small</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Laodicea</b> more prominent - <b>legitimate synagogue</b> and Jewish community</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Hierapolis</b> less so; (misspoke <b>Heliopolis</b>)</li><li class=\"d0\">Primarily <b>Greek</b> with Jewish communities</li><li class=\"d1\">makes Jewish mysticism \"not impossible, but still weird\"</li><li class=\"d1\">that <b>esoteric group</b> taking over synagogues of major cities - \"beyond a fringe\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Far likelier: <b>pagan converts</b> - problems addressed are theirs</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>worship of angels</b> not a problem for a pious Jewish synagogue member</li><li class=\"d0\">Issues = <b>temptations or blind alleys</b> for converts from paganism</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>syncretism</b>: not fully <b>converting your mind</b>, \"misinterpreting Christian teaching\"</li></ul>"}]});
